<section
	class="faq"
	aria-label="Frequently asked questions"
	data-bio="spikes"
	data-bio-group="faq"
>
	<div class="grouphead">
		<span>FAQ</span>
	</div>

	<details class="faqitem">
		<summary>
			<span>What does it do?</span>
			<span class="faqsign" aria-hidden="true"></span>
		</summary>
		<p>
			This site turns human voice into birdsong. It's inspired by <a
				href="https://www.windytan.com/2021/03/speech-to-birdsong-conversion.html"
				>windytan's Blackbird experiment</a
			>
		</p>
	</details>

	<details class="faqitem">
		<summary>
			<span>How does it work?</span>
			<span class="faqsign" aria-hidden="true"></span>
		</summary>
		<p>
			A voice is a stack of harmonics over a fundamental pitch. The
			converter shifts that fundamental down to near 0 Hz, low-pass
			filters it to isolate it, and then uses FM demodulation to track its
			instantaneous frequency. AM demodulation on the same signal gives
			the amplitude envelope. The original harmonics are thrown away. The
			output is a freshly synthesized tone six times higher, shaped by the
			tracked pitch and envelope.
		</p>
		<p>
			That is why it reproduces melody, rhythm and inflection so well but
			not words: only F0 and loudness survive, not the formants that make
			phonemes. Windytan's original ran on a Perl/SoX/csdr pipeline. This
			version ports the same DSP to an AudioWorklet, with extra pitch,
			timbre and trill controls.
		</p>
		<p>
			See source code on <a href="https://github.com/MitPitt/birdspeech"
				>Github</a
			> for details.
		</p>
	</details>

	<details class="faqitem">
		<summary>
			<span>Settings tips?</span>
			<span class="faqsign" aria-hidden="true"></span>
		</summary>
		<p>
			"Voice range" is the most important value, change it to fit your low
			and high vocals. The "auto" button will try to guess it from the
			media file.
		</p>
		<p>
			Hitting "Randomize" is very fun. You can checkbox which values you
			want randomized or kept.
		</p>
	</details>

	<details class="faqitem">
		<summary>
			<span>Voice tips?</span>
			<span class="faqsign" aria-hidden="true"></span>
		</summary>
		<p>
			The best input is exaggerated spoken word, poetry, or slow song
			acapellas. Try and make it rhythmic, like a bird song!
		</p>
		<p>
			The conversion works best with clean vocals and no background noise.
			But you can try adjusting the Equalizer curve to try and focus audio
			range.
		</p>
	</details>

	<details class="faqitem">
		<summary>
			<span>What birds can it do?</span>
			<span class="faqsign" aria-hidden="true"></span>
		</summary>
		<p>
			It can do birds with a clean voice: small singing birds, but also
			some bigger ones like pigeon, cuckoo, owl. It cannot do ducks' croak
			or crows' caw, they would need a different approach.
		</p>
	</details>
</section>
